# DIRECT dependencies for the ROS 2 SDK container image (docker/Dockerfile).
#
# This is the file a human edits. It is NOT what the image installs — that is
# requirements.txt, the fully-resolved transitive closure compiled from this file
# (the standard pip-tools/uv layout: hand-written .in -> generated .txt).
#
# EVERY direct dependency is pinned to an exact version. That is deliberate and it
# is not the same trade-off a library would make:
#
#   - This file builds a container image that drives a physical robot. An
#     unpinned `torch` meant two builds of one commit could ship different
#     inference behaviour, with nothing in the repo recording which.
#   - Pinning here bounds the closure; pinning the closure in requirements.txt is
#     what actually makes two builds of one commit install the same bytes, since
#     that is the file `pip install -r` reads.
#   - The versions below were resolved for the DEPLOYED target: linux/amd64,
#     CPython 3.10 (what `ros:humble-ros-base` provides on the g4dn EC2 instance
#     `make push-image` builds for).
#
# TWO closures are compiled from this one file, because a pin is not a build.
# `torch==2.14.0` is a CUDA wheel on x86_64 and a Grace/sbsa CUDA wheel on aarch64
# that segfaults on import under Docker Desktop on Apple Silicon — one pin, two
# artifacts, one of which does not run:
#
#   requirements.txt        x86_64  — from this file alone.
#   requirements-arm64.txt  aarch64 — from requirements-arm64.in, which is `-r` this
#                                     file plus the torch/torchvision +cpu wheels.
#                                     Read that file's header before touching torch.
#
# Upgrading is an explicit, reviewable act. Edit the pin here, then recompile BOTH
# closures (`make lock`, or with --upgrade to move transitive versions too).
#
# Commit the .in and .txt together. Then scan the result — `make audit` reads the
# compiled files, because pinning buys reproducibility, not the absence of CVEs.
#
# Hashes live in the compiled files, not here: `make lock` passes --generate-hashes
# and every install path passes --require-hashes. Version pins stop drift; they do
# not stop a re-uploaded or tampered artifact, which is what the hashes are for.
# See "Hashes" in PE-REMEDIATION.md.

# --- Robot link (WebRTC to the dog) ------------------------------------------
aiortc==1.15.0
unitree_webrtc_connect==2.2.0
aiohttp==3.14.3

# --- Messaging / config ------------------------------------------------------
paho-mqtt==2.1.0
python-dotenv==1.2.3

# --- LiDAR decode ------------------------------------------------------------
# wasmtime executes external_lib/libvoxel.wasm — see the integrity check in
# go2_robot_sdk/infrastructure/sensors/lidar_decoder.py.
wasmtime==48.0.0
# 0.18.0, not 0.19.0: 0.19 has no wheel for CPython 3.10 on manylinux_2_28, which
# is exactly the interpreter and platform this image is.
open3d==0.18.0

# --- Detection (coco_detector_node) -----------------------------------------
# x86_64 gets the CUDA build from PyPI, which is what the g4dn EC2 target runs
# (coco_device:=cuda). aarch64 CANNOT use these pins — it needs the +cpu wheels in
# requirements-arm64.in. Change one, change the other.
torch==2.14.0
torchvision==0.29.0

# --- Media / crypto ---------------------------------------------------------
opencv-python==4.11.0.86
pydub==0.25.1
# pycryptodome, NOT pycrypto — same `Crypto.*` import namespace, maintained fork.
# Required by unitree-webrtc-connect, which does the Go2 handshake crypto; pinned
# here so the version is ours to control rather than whatever resolves. No code in
# this repository imports `Crypto.*` directly.
pycryptodome==3.23.0

# --- Numerics ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Held on the 1.x line on purpose: the ROS 2 Humble Python packages in this image
# are built against the NumPy 1 C ABI. docker/Dockerfile installs this first for
# the same reason.
numpy==1.26.4

# --- AWS -------------------------------------------------------------------
boto3==1.43.88
requests==2.34.2
